Looking at line 879-880:
```c
/* set the modulus */
rsa->modulus.data = gendat.modulus;
rsa->modulus.len  = kinfo->modulus_length >> 3;
```

If `gendat.modulus` is NULL (because key generation failed silently), then `rsa->modulus.data` will be set to NULL. This is passed back to the caller.

Then in pkcs15-lib.c around line 1617, there's:
```c
r = profile->ops->generate_key(profile, p15card, object, &pubkey_args.key);
```

The `pubkey_args.key` is now a public key with potentially NULL modulus data.

Then later at lines 1631-1637:
```c
pubkey = &pubkey_args.key;
if (!pubkey->alg_id)   {
    pubkey->alg_id = calloc(1, sizeof(struct sc_algorithm_id));
    if (!pubkey->alg_id) {
        r = SC_ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY;
        LOG_TEST_GOTO_ERR(ctx, r, "Can not allocate memory for algorithm id");
    }
    ...
}
```

And at lines 1640-1643:
```c
pubkey_args.id = key_info->id;
r = sc_pkcs15_encode_pubkey(ctx, pubkey, &object->content.value, &object->content.len);
LOG_TEST_GOTO_ERR(ctx, r, "Failed to encode public key");
```

`sc_pkcs15_encode_pubkey` is called with `pubkey` which has `modulus.data = NULL`. This might cause a crash or memory error.


The crash would manifest in `sc_pkcs15_encode_pubkey` or subsequent operations when it tries to process the NULL modulus. However, the fuzzer returned exit code 0 with no crash, so either the test path doesn't reach this code, or there's additional error handling that prevents the crash. I should examine the fuzzer's input format more carefully—it expects a profile configuration followed by reader data that could be affecting how this code path is executed.

